Remove the "Sync and Google services" section and move the search-suggestion
toggle to the Search page.

Settings > Your browser still carried the whole sign-in/sync block: a
"Sync and Google services" row, a "Google services" row (whichever of the two
is live depends on the ReplaceSyncPromosWithSignInPromos flag - exactly one is
always visible), and a "Manage your Google Account" row linking out to
myaccount.google.com. None of it does anything in a de-Googled build: GAIA is
disabled, so sign-in and sync can never start, and the account link points at
a service the browser deliberately cannot talk to.

What goes:

  - people_page.html: the sync-setup, google-services and manage-google-account
    rows. importDataDialogTrigger stays - importing bookmarks and settings is
    a genuinely useful, entirely local feature.

  - people_page_index.html: the sync, syncControls and googleServices views.
    Removing a row without removing the view it points at is not safe: settings
    search walks every child view, and on a hit it calls the parent's
    getAssociatedControlFor(viewId), which asserts that the row exists. Rows
    and views therefore have to go together. The matching cases in
    people_page_index.ts's currentRouteChanged() go too, so a hand-typed
    chrome://settings/syncSetup falls through to the default branch and simply
    stays on the parent view instead of asserting inside cr-view-manager.

  - people_page.ts: the handlers, focus-config entries and
    getAssociatedControlFor cases that referenced the removed rows.
    Chromium's ts_library builds with noUnusedLocals, so a private method left
    behind with no caller is a compile error, not dead code. The
    OpenWindowProxyImpl import is now only reachable from the ChromeOS branch
    of onProfileClick_, so it is wrapped in the matching <if expr>.

Nothing of substance is lost with the googleServices view: its entire body is
<settings-personalization-options>, and on an unbranded ungoogled build that
element renders almost nothing - the URL-collection toggle is already deleted
by extra/ungoogled-chromium/remove-uneeded-ui.patch, metrics reporting and
spell-check-service are behind _google_chrome, the Chrome-signin choice is
behind <if expr="False">, and the price-email toggle needs sync.

The one control there that does work de-Googled is "Improve search
suggestions" (prefs.search.suggest_enabled): suggestions are fetched from the
default search engine's suggest_url, and aerium-search-engines.patch gives
DuckDuckGo one. So it is relocated rather than deleted - to Settings > Search
engine, directly under the engine picker it depends on, which is where people
look for it anyway.

Three things that relocation needs:

  - search_page.ts declared `prefs: Object` but did not use PrefsMixin, unlike
    every settings page that hosts a writable toggle. Without the mixin's
    `notify: true` on prefs, a change made by settings-toggle-button would
    never propagate back up to <settings-prefs> and the pref would not be
    written - a toggle that flips and then silently forgets. PrefsMixin is
    composed in innermost, matching privacy_page.ts and appearance_page.ts,
    and the local prefs property/declaration is dropped in favour of the
    mixin's.

  - '../controls/settings_toggle_button.js' is imported so the element is
    defined on this page.

  - the guest-mode guard the toggle used to carry (pageVisibility.privacy
    .searchPrediction) is not carried over. It existed because the privacy
    page is hidden wholesale for guest profiles on desktop; the search page is
    not, and search.suggest_enabled is a perfectly ordinary profile pref.
    Dropping it also drops personalization_options.ts's last use of
    pageVisibility, so those imports go with it.

The strings are reworded in place rather than replaced with new IDS_ names.
IDS_SETTINGS_SUGGEST_PREF and IDS_SETTINGS_SUGGEST_PREF_DESC are already
mapped to searchSuggestPref/searchSuggestPrefDesc in
settings_localized_strings_provider.cc, and $i18n{} resolves against the whole
settings data source regardless of which page the template lives on, so
reusing them keeps this patch out of two heavily-patched files for no
behavioural difference. New wording drops the "improve" framing and says
plainly where the suggestions come from.
